  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The work is a localized qualitative and historical case study focused on a single neighborhood conflict in New York City.",
      "what": "This book analyzes Columbia University's controversial deployment of eminent domain to acquire property in West Harlem, examining how elite institutions shape urban space. It traces the spatial and racial politics of redevelopment, documenting how institutional power restructures local neighborhoods.",
      "why": "It provides a detailed case study of how private universities act as powerful real estate engines, exposing the mechanisms through which elite institutional expansion displaces historically Black communities under the guise of urban renewal."
    }
